From: Alexandr Mironov <7062352+alexandr-mironov@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8150-xiaomi-vayu: keep the QLINK supplies on

TESTED 2026-08-18, NO EFFECT. Kept here so nobody repeats the experiment.
With ldo3 (PM8150L) and ldo18 (PM8150) held on and confirmed enabled by
the regulator framework, bringing the radio online still ends in the same
RFLM assert, and the RF scheduler journal (log 0x1843) still shows one RF
device answering and the rest returning nothing. The QLINK supplies as the
AP sees them are not what keeps the front end silent.

Original description follows.

Bringing the modem radio online on vayu kills the modem within a second:

    qcom_q6v5_pas 4080000.remoteproc: watchdog received:
      rflm_diag_error.cc:368:RFLM@qsf_hl_seq.c:119
      Assertion (rflm_qlnk_ls_retry_cnt < 2) failed

RFLM is the RF link manager and QLINK is the bus between the modem and the
RF front end. The modem's own DIAG log (RF scheduler journal, log 0x1843)
shows why: of the ten RF devices it tries to bring up, exactly one answers
and nine never return a result to any command; the modem retries until the
retry counter trips. Every command to the silent devices goes out over
QLINK.

On the AP side the QLINK supplies are ldo5 on PM8150 (vdda_qlink_lv,
vdda_qlink_lv_ck) and ldo3 on PM8150L (vdda_qlink_hv_ck) - the board file
already labels them that way. ldo5 happens to stay on because the USB PHY
uses it. ldo3 has no consumer, and RPMh reports it disabled:

    ldo3 (pm8150l): state=disabled uses=0

The vendor tree also defines pm8150_l18 (880-912 mV, RF), which the
mainline board file omits entirely.

Keep ldo3 (pm8150l) on and add ldo18 (pm8150) as always-on, matching what
the sibling sm8150 boards (raphael, hdk, kumano) describe for the same
rails. There is no AP-side consumer to attach these to: the modem votes
for its RF power through RPMh on its own, and on this board those votes
evidently do not reach the rails.

Tested on Xiaomi POCO X3 Pro (vayu_global, M2102J20SG), Huaxing panel,
kernel 7.0.0-sm8150 from sm8150/7.0-wip.
---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi     | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
@@ -367,6 +367,14 @@
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <3008000>;
 			regulator-initial-mode = <RPMH_REGULATOR_MODE_HPM>;
 		};
+
+		/* Present in the vendor tree (pm8150_l18, 880-912 mV); RF supply. */
+		vreg_l18a_0p8: ldo18 {
+			regulator-min-microvolt = <880000>;
+			regulator-max-microvolt = <912000>;
+			regulator-initial-mode = <RPMH_REGULATOR_MODE_HPM>;
+			regulator-always-on;
+		};
 	};
 
 	pm8150l-rpmh-regulators {
@@ -430,6 +438,13 @@
 			regulator-min-microvolt = <1200000>;
 			regulator-max-microvolt = <1200000>;
 			regulator-initial-mode = <RPMH_REGULATOR_MODE_HPM>;
+			/*
+			 * Powers the HV side of QLINK, the bus between the modem
+			 * and the RF front end. Nothing on the AP side consumes
+			 * it and RPMh reports it off; the modem then fails to
+			 * bring up every RF device but one and asserts in RFLM.
+			 */
+			regulator-always-on;
 		};
 
 		vddpx_5:
